 ## Version history
 
+- **1.5** (unreleased) — a build flag, `PF_MIDI_OUT_LANE_ABSOLUTE` (default `0`), for a composition in which a lane IS the hand: the private exhibit build keeps one position per knob and hands it to every pattern as the knob's lane, so the picture and the sound come from one number. With the flag, a knob whose lane is active in the finished frame is sent as CC `24–27` = `round(lane × 127)` — always absolute, `outMode`/`outDiv`/`outMul` do not apply to it — paced like any knob (at most one per 50 ms, only on change, the last value always sent), and a host that arrives hears each such knob's value right after the Program Change (a knob whose lane is off in that one frame, the frame of a press, on the next). A knob with no lane is reported exactly as before. `/api/status` `midi` gains `"outLaneAbs": true` in such a build only. Without the flag every edition sends byte for byte what it sent before — the Audio edition's microphone lanes stay unreported.
+- **1.4** (unreleased) — Program Change is resent, unchanged, when a host arrives (an RTP-MIDI session joins; a USB host mounts or wakes), so a DAW that connects after boot or after a drop learns the current pattern without anyone turning K4. Knob CCs are not resent. A composition may name a host to invite with `PF_MIDI_DEFAULT_HOST`, used only while no host has ever been stored; `POST /api/midi?host=` still sets or clears it and the stored answer wins. **Fix:** `rtpPeers` counts RTP-MIDI sessions by SSRC instead of counting reports. AppleMIDI 3.5.0 can report one session joining twice (a host answering two re-sent data invitations) and leaving once; the count then stayed at 1 with no session up, and a panel with a `host` never invited it again until it rebooted. A repeated join is not an arrival and does not resend the Program Change. The end of an invitation the panel gave up on no longer takes a live session off the count, including one that carries that host's SSRC (the panel recognises the give-up by the library's exception raised just before it), and an unanswered invitation no longer releases held notes. The count names hosts, not sessions: one host holding two sessions counts once, so output is no longer gated on it — the transport sends whenever the library has a participant to send to. The panel never opens a second invitation to a host while the first may still be open, which could leave two sessions with one host. Build settings for a panel whose host is a box that only listens: `PF_MIDI_SESSION_LOCKED` (above), `PF_MIDI_FIRST_INVITE_DELAY_MS` (the first invitation that long after the network is up; default the full 20 s, the head start a computer that re-joins a remembered panel needs) and `PF_MIDI_REINVITE_ON_REJOIN` (when the Wi-Fi comes back, every session is ended and the host invited again after that delay, instead of waiting up to ~70 s for clock sync to give up), `PF_MIDI_INVITE_ATTEMPTS` (how many times one invitation is sent, a second apart, before it is given up; default the library's 13 — a host that ignores a repeat of an invitation it already answered can only be reached again by a new one, so fewer means a lost answer costs seconds, not most of a minute; the one-invitation window shrinks with it), `PF_MIDI_REINVITE_AFTER_END_MS` (the host invited again that long after an invitation is given up or the last session ends, instead of on the 20 s period; on a session's end everything the library still lists is ended first) and `PF_MIDI_WIFI_HEAL_MS` (after that long on a Wi-Fi link that is up with no session and at least two invitations unanswered, the panel asks the core to reconnect the Wi-Fi, at most once per `PF_MIDI_WIFI_HEAL_GAP_MS`, default 2 minutes), `PF_MIDI_REINVITE_MS` (the re-invite period itself, default 20 s; still never while the last invitation may be open), `PF_MIDI_REINVITE_ON_REJECTION` (an invitation the host refuses with a NO, or ends with a BY before it has joined, is over at once and the host is invited again after `PF_MIDI_REINVITE_AFTER_END_MS` — AppleMIDI drops a refused invitation without telling the panel, which then waited for the re-invite period; a NO does not count as unanswered for the self-heal) and `PF_MIDI_SYNC_HEARTBEAT_MS` / `PF_MIDI_SYNC_RETRY_MS` / `PF_MIDI_SYNC_UNANSWERED` (a clock sync every N ms once a session has settled, an unanswered one sent again after N ms, the session ended when N in a row go unanswered; the library's 10 s / 10 s / 6 keep a session whose host has gone for up to ~70 s, in which nothing is invited). No published edition sets them. No change to the map.
